Task 1: Viewing SELinux Contexts with ls -Z
Subtask 1.1: View file contexts
Create a test file:

touch /tmp/testfile.txt
View its SELinux context:

ls -Z /tmp/testfile.txt
Expected output:

unconfined_u:object_r:user_tmp_t:s0 /tmp/testfile.txt
View directory context:

ls -Zd /tmp
Subtask 1.2: Understand context components
The context has four colon-separated parts:

User: unconfined_u
Role: object_r
Type: user_tmp_t
Level: s0
Key Concept: The type is most important for access control decisions.

Task 2: Changing Contexts with chcon
Subtask 2.1: Change file context temporarily
Create a web directory:

sudo mkdir /web
View its default context:

ls -Zd /web
Change to httpd context:

sudo chcon -t httpd_sys_content_t /web
Verify the change:

ls -Zd /web
Expected type should now be httpd_sys_content_t

Subtask 2.2: Restore default context
Restore default contexts:

sudo restorecon -v /web
Verify restoration:

ls -Zd /web
Task 3: Interpreting Audit Logs and AVC Denials
Subtask 3.1: Generate an AVC denial
Create a test scenario:

sudo mkdir -p /web/html
sudo touch /web/html/index.html
sudo chcon -t user_home_t /web/html/index.html
sudo systemctl start httpd
Try accessing the file through web server (will trigger denial):

curl http://localhost/html/index.html
Subtask 3.2: Analyze the denial
Check SELinux denials:

sudo ausearch -m avc -ts recent
For more readable output:

sudo sealert -a /var/log/audit/audit.log
Look for:

Source context (process trying to access)
Target context (object being accessed)
Denied permission
Subtask 3.3: Resolve the denial
Apply correct context:

sudo chcon -t httpd_sys_content_t /web/html/index.html
Verify access works now:

curl http://localhost/html/index.html
Troubleshooting Tips
If sealert isn't working, ensure the setroubleshoot service is running:

sudo systemctl start setroubleshoot
For persistent context changes, use semanage fcontext instead of chcon:

sudo semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t "/web(/.*)?"
sudo restorecon -Rv /web
To temporarily put SELinux in permissive mode for troubleshooting:

sudo setenforce 0
